/**
  @page guides-variational Variational formulations in Rodin
  @brief Translate a weak equation into trial fields, test fields, forms, and assembly.

  A Rodin problem is built from the same objects as a weak formulation, including spaces,
  unknown fields, test functions, known data, and integrals. This guide explains
  their roles in code. The @ref guides-weak-formulations "weak formulation guide"
  supplies the derivation; the @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial"
  provides a complete runnable solve.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-variational-weak Write the weak equation

  For homogeneous Dirichlet Poisson, the weak equation is
  @f[
    a(u,v)=\ell(v),\qquad
    a(u,v)=\int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dx,\qquad
    \ell(v)=\int_\Omega fv\,dx.
  @f]
  Rodin writes this as a residual @f$a(u,v)-\ell(v)=0@f$, together with the
  essential boundary condition.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-form

  Here `mesh` is the previously constructed unit-square mesh. `P1` selects a
  continuous, piecewise affine space. Trial fields, data and forms then define the discrete equation on that space.

  @section guides-variational-fields Unknown fields and prescribed data

  | Object | Role during assembly |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | `TrialFunction u(vh)` | Unknown field expanded in trial basis functions |
  | `TestFunction v(vh)` | Test basis functions indexing the equations |
  | `RealFunction f` | Known scalar coefficient or source |
  | `VectorFunction g` | Known vector-valued data |
  | `GridFunction field(vh)` | A field represented by finite element coefficients |

  Trial and test functions are symbolic until integration evaluates their basis
  functions on a cell. A solved trial function exposes its finite element field
  through `u.getSolution()`. A separately constructed grid function may instead
  hold initial data, a previous time step, or another known coefficient.

  Assignment of a known function to a grid function applies the space's DOF
  functionals. For nodal P1 this gives interpolation. An @f$L^2@f$ projection is defined by a
  mass-matrix problem; see @ref guides-numerical-contracts.

  @section guides-variational-forms Construct the forms

  The number and role of symbolic arguments determine what an integral produces.

  | Expression | Role over real fields |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | `Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))` | Diffusion bilinear form |
  | `Integral(c * u, v)` | Weighted mass bilinear form |
  | `Integral(f, v)` | Domain load linear form |
  | `BoundaryIntegral(g, v).over(label)` | Load on selected boundary facets |

  A coefficient such as `c` must be defined before forming the expression. It supplies known data during assembly. With scalar data, these forms represent products;
  with vector or tensor data, the corresponding inner products must have
  compatible shapes.

  @subsection guides-variational-forms-assembly Residual signs

  The matrix and load for basis functions @f$\phi_j@f$ and @f$\psi_i@f$ have
  entries @f$A_{ij}=a(\phi_j,\psi_i)@f$ and @f$b_i=\ell(\psi_i)@f$ before
  essential elimination. A linear form subtracted in the residual becomes a
  positive right-hand-side load.

  Each bilinear term retains its sign from the weak equation. The Helmholtz residual
  contains a negative mass term, while the mixed Stokes residual contains signed
  velocity–pressure coupling blocks.

  @subsection guides-variational-forms-named Select named forms

  @ref Rodin::Variational::MassForm "MassForm",
  @ref Rodin::Variational::DiffusionForm "DiffusionForm",
  @ref Rodin::Variational::HelmholtzForm "HelmholtzForm", and
  @ref Rodin::Variational::LinearElasticityForm "LinearElasticityForm" expose
  common cell operators. For compatible spaces and the corresponding quadrature
  order, they represent the same discrete forms as their integral expressions.
  Their reference pages specify supported field types and assembly backends.

  The following forms use scalar trial and test functions with previously
  defined coefficients.

  @code{.cpp}
  MassForm mass(c, u, v);
  DiffusionForm diffusion(a, u, v);
  HelmholtzForm helmholtz(a, c, u, v);
  @endcode

  Named forms restrict their cell domain with `.over(attribute)`. Their
  coefficients, boundary conditions and quadrature are specified by the
  mathematical problem.

  @section guides-variational-operators Choose operators by field shape

  | Expression | Meaning |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | `Grad(u)` for a scalar field | Gradient vector |
  | `Jacobian(w)` for a vector field | Matrix with entries @f$\partial_jw_i@f$ |
  | `Div(w)` | Scalar divergence @f$\sum_i\partial_iw_i@f$ |
  | `Transpose(Jacobian(w))` | Transposed gradient matrix |
  | `Mult(Jacobian(w), velocity)` | Directional derivative @f$(\mathrm{velocity}\cdot\nabla)w@f$ |

  For linear elasticity, the symmetric strain is
  @f$\varepsilon(w)=\tfrac12(\nabla w+\nabla w^T)@f$. Omitting the factor
  @f$1/2@f$ changes the strain energy. The
  @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "elasticity tutorial" uses
  `LinearElasticityIntegral` to include the complete Lamé-parameter form.

  @section guides-variational-complex Complex test convention

  Over complex fields, Rodin's bilinear-form interface is sesquilinear. It is linear in the trial argument and conjugate-linear in the test argument. Thus
  `Integral(c * u, v)` represents
  @f$\int_\Omega c\,u\overline v\,dx@f$. The coefficient retains its original complex value.
  The corresponding discrete action is @f$y^*Ax@f$.

  The form language and assembly apply this conjugation once. Backend transfer
  preserves the assembled entries. Hermitian symmetry depends on the particular
  form and coefficients.
  See the @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz "complex Helmholtz example".

  @section guides-variational-bc Apply boundary data

  An essential condition is appended as a `DirichletBC`. A prescribed natural
  load is part of the residual through a `BoundaryIntegral`. Their selections
  have different roles. `.on(label)` selects constrained DOFs, while
  `.over(label)` selects an integration region.

  The @ref guides-boundary-conditions "boundary conditions guide" develops a
  checked mixed-boundary example, including the normal-derivative sign. It also
  explains periodic identification and the normalization of nullspaces.

  @section guides-variational-mixed Couple multiple fields

  A mixed problem lists all trial fields, followed by all test fields. In the
  @ref examples-pdes-stokes "Stokes example", velocity, pressure, and a global
  pressure-mean multiplier have distinct spaces.

  @snippet PDEs/Stokes.cpp stokes-form

  `P0g` supplies a single global constant. The multiplier imposes zero mean pressure as a constraint. Quadratic velocity and
  linear pressure form the Taylor–Hood pair.

  @section guides-variational-dg Couple discontinuous fields

  A discontinuous method needs numerical coupling between neighboring cells.
  `Jump`, `Average`, `FaceIntegral`, and `InterfaceIntegral` supply the relevant
  trace and facet operations; the @ref guides-interfaces-dg "interface and DG guide"
  defines their side and normal conventions.

  A P0 field has zero cellwise gradient, so its diffusion coupling must be supplied
  through the DG formulation. A consistent method specifies numerical fluxes,
  stabilization, boundary treatment and the mesh/degree scaling of penalty terms.

  @section guides-variational-solvers Assemble, solve, and inspect

  A configured solver retains a reference to its problem. On the first solve,
  the problem is assembled before the algebraic solver runs. The Poisson example
  checks the result before export.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-solve

  Reassigning a `Problem` body marks its assembly stale. When known field data change, explicitly reassemble or rebuild the problem. A new mesh or a different finite
  element space requires a new problem and linear system, with fresh associated
  solver objects. See @ref guides-numerical-contracts for the lifetime rules.

  @section guides-variational-output Export the solved field

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-export

  `u.getSolution()` supplies the computed grid function for export. Keep referenced HDF5 files alongside the XDMF descriptor.
  The @ref guides-io "I/O guide" covers multiple fields and time series.

  @section guides-variational-complete Examples

  The @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial" combines these steps in one
  program. The @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz "Helmholtz",
  @ref examples-pdes-stokes "Stokes", and
  @ref examples-pdes-navierstokes "Navier–Stokes" examples show how a different
  weak equation changes matrix properties and solver requirements. Use the
  @ref guides-solvers "solver guide" to interpret those requirements.
*/
